The maps $t_m$ are equivariant for relabeling. For every strata expression $b$, they satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
 t(\bar\psi_i b)&=\lambda_i t(b),
 &t(\bar\kappa_j b)&=0, \label{eq:detector-bars}\\
 t(\bar D_{ij}b)&=\Delta_{ij}(1)t(b). \label{eq:detector-pair}
\end{aligned}$$ More generally, gluing along $D_T$ integrates the entire genus-zero input and duplicates the root-side attaching slot into the labels of $T$.

On $C^{2b+2}$, one has $$\label{eq:relative-pfaffian}
 \sum_{\mathcal M}
   \prod_{\{i,j\}\in\mathcal M}\eta_{ij}=0,$$ where $\mathcal M$ ranges over the perfect matchings of $\{1,\ldots,2b+2\}$.

Suppose $b\geq5$ and $\dim S<\chi-1$. Let $(w_\alpha)_{\alpha\in I}$ be any finite collection of vectors in the odd symplectic standard space $W_b$ whose span is isotropic. For any matrix of scalars $\gamma=(\gamma_{\alpha\beta})_{\alpha,\beta\in I}$, put $$\label{eq:surface-tensor}
 T_\gamma=
 \sum_{\substack{\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_3\in I\\
                  \beta_1,\beta_2,\beta_3\in I}}
 \left(\prod_{j=1}^3\gamma_{\alpha_j\beta_j}\right)
 \varphi(w_{\alpha_1},w_{\alpha_2},w_{\alpha_3})
 \varphi(w_{\beta_1},w_{\beta_2},w_{\beta_3}).$$ Then $$\label{eq:surface-bound}
 T_\gamma^{\,t_0}=0\qquad\text{in }\mathfrak B.$$ The assertion remains valid after any characteristic-zero scalar extension of the tensor formalism.

The unrestricted low rank-one Quot scheme is classically $$\operatorname{Div}_{P}(X/S)\times_S X^{[r_{\mathrm{low}}]}_{/S},$$ where the divisor scheme permits its line to vary in the prescribed Picard data. Its virtual class is the divisor virtual class times the smooth relative point-Hilbert class, capped with $$\label{eq:point-obstruction}
 e(F_{\mathrm{low}}),\qquad
 F_i=\left(\pi_*\mathcal O_{Z_i}(K-\mathcal C_i)\right)^\vee.$$

There is a series $c(q,x_e,x_m)$ such that $$\label{eq:theta-exponential}
 \mathcal E(\Theta)
 =
 \mathcal E(\Theta|_{p=0})
 \exp\bigl(c(q,x_e,x_m)p^3/3!\bigr).$$ Moreover, let $c_H(q)$ be the coefficient of $M_3(H)$, with the factor $p^3/3!$ removed, in the one-moment part of $\log\Theta(q,p,0,0)$. Then $$\label{eq:shift-derivative}
 [x_e]c(q,x_e,0)=-c_H(q).$$
